[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write up the proof properly.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, let me reconsider whether the problem might be more subtle. The [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mproblem says "Evaluate the integral ... as $X \to \infty$ along the path $z = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\infty + iy, y \in [0,1]$."[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mI think "along the path $z = \infty + iy$" describes the contour $C_3$, and "as [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$X \to \infty$" is the asymptotic regime. The answer is that the integral [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mevaluates to 0.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis makes sense in the context of steepest descent: when you deform contours, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe integrals along arcs at infinity vanish, and only the steepest descent [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontours through saddle points contribute. The contour $C_3$ at $\text{Re}(z) = [0m[38;2;124;124;124m+\infty$ is such a vanishing contour.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write the proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mProof:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet $g(z) = z + iz - z^2/2$. On the contour $C_3$, we parameterize $z = x + iy$ [0m[38;2;124;124;124mwhere $x \to +\infty$ and $y \in [0,1]$, with $dz = i\,dy$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWe compute:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$g(x+iy) = (x+iy) + i(x+iy) - \frac{(x+iy)^2}{2}$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$= x + iy + ix - y - \frac{x^2 - y^2 + 2ixy}{2}$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$= \left(x - y - \frac{x^2}{2} + \frac{y^2}{2}\right) + i\left(y + x - [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mxy\right)$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe real part is:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\text{Re}\, g(x+iy) = -\frac{(x-1)^2}{2} + \frac{(y-1)^2}{2}$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m(completing the square in both $x$ and $y$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $y \in [0,1]$, we have $(y-1)^2 \leq 1$, so:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\text{Re}\, g(x+iy) \leq -\frac{(x-1)^2}{2} + \frac{1}{2}$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\left|\int_{C_3} e^{Xg(z)}\,dz\right| = \left|i\int_0^1 [0m
[38;2;124;124;124me^{Xg(x+iy)}\,dy\right| \leq \int_0^1 e^{X\,\text{Re}\,g(x+iy)}\,dy$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\leq \int_0^1 e^{X[-(x-1)^2/2 + 1/2]}\,dy = e^{-X(x-1)^2/2 + X/2}$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAs $x \to \infty$, $(x-1)^2 \to \infty$, so $e^{-X(x-1)^2/2 + X/2} \to 0$ for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124many $X > 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\int_{C_3} e^{X(z+iz-z^2/2)}\,dz = 0$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis holds for all $X > 0$, and in particular as $X \to \infty$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m### PROOF COMPLETE[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mWait, I should double-check the completing the square.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$\text{Re}\, g = x - y - x^2/2 + y^2/2$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$= -(x^2/2 - x) + (y^2/2 - y)$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$= -\frac{1}{2}(x^2 - 2x) + \frac{1}{2}(y^2 - 2y)$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$= -\frac{1}{2}((x-1)^2 - 1) + \frac{1}{2}((y-1)^2 - 1)$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$= -\frac{(x-1)^2}{2} + \frac{1}{2} + \frac{(y-1)^2}{2} - \frac{1}{2}$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$= -\frac{(x-1)^2}{2} + \frac{(y-1)^2}{2}$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mYes, that's correct. The proof is solid.[0m

